    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 212249\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n349 PM PDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Showers and thunderstorms expected well into the evening, \n  particularly in the southern Great Basin.\n\n* Near normal temperatures early this week, warming back up to\n  above normal mid week into next weekend. \n\n* Potential increase in moisture for Mohave County late week\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nThe latest satellite imagery shows the upper low has moved onshore \nin the central coast of California. The low will work its way across \nNevada overnight before exiting the state Tuesday afternoon. So far \ntoday there have been scattered showers with light precipitation and \na few thunderstorms. As the low moves further inland this evening \nthe most widespread coverage of showers and thunderstorms will be \nover Esmeralda, Nye and Lincoln Counties as well as over the higher \nterrain of Clark County. Average rain totals will be around 0.25\" \nwith a few localized amounts pushing 0.50\". Moisture continues to \nslowly increase with the latest 18Z sounding in Las Vegas indicating \nnearly an inch of PW and decent saturation down to about 700 mb. \nDCAPE values are modest but will support some stronger winds gusts \nin the 40 to 50 mph range with any stronger storm. There will be \nsufficient effective shear in place to support some stronger storms \nfrom the higher terrain of Clark County and into Lincoln County this \nevening. With the cold core moving across Nevada this evening shower \nand thunderstorms will likely continue well into the evening and \novernight, particularly in Lincoln County. \n\nSlightly cooler air will be in place on Tuesday behind the upper low \nand most locations will see 2 to 3 degrees of cooling in comparison \nto todays highs. The low will eject fairly quickly to the northeast \non Tuesday morning but there may be a few lingering showers into the \nearly afternoon in Lincoln County. Temperatures will rebound on \nWednesday and remain at or slightly above normal through the \nweekend. \n\nThe flow in generally will be a dry and southwesterly but the \nensembles continue to indicate a modest increase in moisture into at \nleast Mohave County on Thursday and Friday. As the initial low exits \nNevada tomorrow, a piece of energy breaks off and lingers off the \nnorthern coast of the Baja and it's that piece of energy that helps \nto increase the moisture. As it moves inland on Friday it takes with \nit whatever increase in moisture it provided as it exits.  \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast\nPackage...Showers and isolated thunderstorms will continue \ndrifting northward across the area, and while a majority of the \nactivity will focus across the higher terrain surrounding the \nValley, showers remain possible through around midnight across \nlower elevations. The main concern will be erratic and gusty winds\nassociated with convection through late evening with periods of \nvariability expected, and thus, confidence in the wind forecast is\nlow. Mid to late evening, a northwesterly wind shift is expected \nto arrive, accompanied by sustained speeds around 15KT with gusts \nup to 25KT through around midnight. Thereafter, showers and storms\nwill move northward with gusts quickly diminishing and winds \ndropping to 8KT or less. VFR conditions are expected to continue, \nwith SCT-BKN ceilings around 10-15kft through tonight that will \nimprove gradually on Tuesday with clear skies returning late in \nthe period.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms will increase in coverage and drift \nnorthward through tonight, especially across the southern Great \nBasin. Highest confidence for potential thunderstorm impacts is at\nKBIH in the northern Owens Valley within the first couple hours \nof the forecast period, with erratic gusty winds, lightning, and \nbriefly lower ceilings expected in the vicinity of any convection.\nConvective activity will also shift eastward tonight, lingering \nacross Lincoln County through Tuesday morning. Winds this evening \nand tonight will be dependent on convective activity, but overall \noutside of these influences, southerly to southeasterly gusts will\ndiminish this evening as sustained speeds drop to around 10KT or \nless overnight onward. Exceptions include the Las Vegas Valley, \nwhere a period of gusty northwesterly winds is expected mid- \nthrough late evening, and the western Mojave Desert/KDAG, where \nwinds remain westerly with gusts to 25KT developing this evening \ninto tonight. VFR conditions will continue with SCT-BKN ceilings \naround 10-15kft, possibly lower in the vicinity of showers and \nstorms.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Czyzyk\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
